---
import ExerciseLayout from '../layouts/ExerciseLayout.astro';
import { LINKS } from '../data/links';
---
<ExerciseLayout
  title="Front Squat 1RM Calculator — Estimate Your Front Squat Max"
  description="Estimate your front squat one rep max from a set of 1–12 reps. Six formulas with the range between them, what counts as a clean rep, and how to fail safely."
  path="/front-squat-calculator/"
  name="Front Squat Calculator"
  h1="Front Squat One Rep Max (1RM) Calculator"
  lead="Estimate your front squat max from a recent set — no max attempt needed. Enter the weight and the clean reps you completed with the bar held on your shoulders."
  exercise="front-squat"
  lift="Front squat"
  example={{ weight: 185, reps: 3, unit: 'lb' }}
  related={[LINKS.squat, LINKS.percent, LINKS.warmup, LINKS.increase]}
  sources={['lesuer', 'reynolds', 'epley']}
>
  <section aria-labelledby="fs-estimate-heading">
    <h2 id="fs-estimate-heading">How to estimate your front squat max</h2>
    <p class="answer">
      Front squat a weight you can lift for 2–5 clean reps, stop when the next rep would lose your
      upper-back position, and enter the weight and reps above. For example, 185 lb × 3 estimates a
      front squat max of roughly 196–211 lb.
    </p>
    <p>
      The validation studies we cite tested the back squat, not the front squat, so expect more
      individual variation. Short, heavy sets work best: front squat sets often end because the
      upper back rounds and the bar starts to roll forward, not because the legs are done, and
      that happens sooner as sets get longer.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="fs-vs-heading">
    <h2 id="fs-vs-heading">Front squat vs back squat</h2>
    <p>
      The front squat is a different lift, not a lighter version of the back squat. The bar sits
      on the front of the shoulders, the torso stays more upright, and the upper back has to hold
      the position. Most lifters front squat less than they back squat, but how much less varies a
      lot from person to person.
    </p>
    <p>
      Because the gap is so individual, do not predict one from the other. Estimate your front squat
      from front squat sets, and your back squat with the <a href="/squat-calculator/">squat calculator</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="fs-rep-heading">
    <h2 id="fs-rep-heading">What counts as a clean front squat rep</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Same depth every rep.</strong> Cutting depth on the last reps inflates the estimate.</li>
      <li><strong>Elbows stay up.</strong> If the elbows drop and the bar slides forward, the rep is not clean.</li>
      <li><strong>The bar stays on the shoulders</strong> from start to finish, with the torso upright.</li>
      <li><strong>Same rack position as your heavy sets</strong> — clean grip, cross-arm or straps — throughout the set.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="fs-safety-heading">
    <h2 id="fs-safety-heading">Front squat safety for heavy sets</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Squat inside a rack with the safety arms set just below your lowest position.</li>
      <li>If a rep fails, let the bar roll forward off your shoulders onto the safeties and step back. Do not try to hold on to a bar that is falling forward.</li>
      <li>For a true max attempt, follow a structured protocol: see <a href="/guides/how-to-test-1rm/">how to test your 1RM safely</a>.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="fs-faq-heading">
    <h2 id="fs-faq-heading">Front squat max: common questions</h2>
    <h3>How do I calculate my front squat max by hand?</h3>
    <p>
      Use the Epley formula: <strong>1RM = weight × (1 + reps ÷ 30)</strong>. For 185 lb × 3:
      185 × (1 + 3 ÷ 30) = <strong>203.5 lb</strong>. The calculator runs five more published
      formulas and shows the range between them.
    </p>
    <h3>Does the clean grip or cross-arm grip change the estimate?</h3>
    <p>
      The formulas do not know your grip. The estimate applies to the rack position you used for
      the set, so keep the same grip when you use the number to plan training.
    </p>
    <h3>Can I use my front squat to predict my back squat?</h3>
    <p>
      Not reliably. The difference between the two lifts varies a lot between lifters, so test or
      estimate each one from its own sets.
    </p>
  </section>
</ExerciseLayout>
